{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1448603-how-masquerade-is-kept-in-wtf","id":1448603,"title":"How \"masquerade\" is kept in WtF?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-15T11:06:30-07:00","id":1448603,"title":"How \"masquerade\" is kept in WtF?","content":"I'm preparing for a chronicle in WtF 2e, and while re-reading corebook and supplementary material (including custom monsters and so on), I've understood that I don't understand how exactly mankind is kept ignorant about Shadow and Uratha, given the immense number of murders which are linked to those. I mean, almost every opponent in the line kills people on regular basis - Hosts, Claimed, rogue spirits, Pure, idigam, etc. If the Forsaken kept the upper hand or had some kind of agreement with those opponents, then I can imagine some coordinated effort to keep all that stuff underground, but most antagonists don't have any desire to do that. This doesn't even take into account how Forsaken themselves can screw things up. Like, imagine pack succumbing to Kuruth in the middle of the city - something which is likely to happen sooner or later in the history of setting. Even if thousands of people succumb to Lunacy, amount of material evidence left behind in slaughter would be too great to play the good old &quot;terrorist attack&quot; card. How do you treat this issue in your games?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11880-dark-archon","name":"Dark Archon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11880_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"348"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T12:15:27-07:00","id":1448612,"title":"","content":"Lunacy and the existing tendency of the Herd to say nothing and drink to forget. &quot;What have you forgotten?&quot; is one of the standard Integrity questions for a reason.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T13:38:01-07:00","id":1448615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dark Archon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1448603-how-masquerade-is-kept-in-wtf#post1448603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm preparing for a chronicle in WtF 2e, and while re-reading corebook and supplementary material (including custom monsters and so on), I've understood that I don't understand how exactly mankind is kept ignorant about Shadow and Uratha, given the immense number of murders which are linked to those. I mean, almost every opponent in the line kills people on regular basis - Hosts, Claimed, rogue spirits, Pure, idigam, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Claimed and rogue spirits and even Pure, idigam, and hosts don't kill people on a regular basis, so there's that. Spirits, including claimed and idigam, don't need to kill to get anything, and mass murder tends to limit their lifespan. Hosts are the only ones that -do- need to eat people but this is after they've incorporated themselves into a human body and are generally intelligent, at least as much as a mortal, and their general ingrained cowardice is likely to help them find ways to do it out of sight. The beshilu from the fiction, for instance, have managed to obtain the body of a police officer and so in addition to having access to people who might otherwise be forgotten (and the capability to hide their crimes from notice and send authorities after anyone who may investigate) they end up causing more damage peripherally. The beshilu don't run around eating everyone in sight, they weaken the Gauntlet, and attract certain kinds of spirits. These spirits, in turn, don't manifest and start killing people, they instead latch onto them and use them as carriers for plague and sickness. A spirit can do this for years while traveling through every large city in the globe and never be able to be seen, as they can do this just lingering invisible in Twilight. While the Pure may care less about being murderous towards humans, they are bound by the same effective oath, and they benefit from being predators within society, and not on YouTube exposing their secrets and potentially incurring the wrath of every other werewolf in existence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the Forsaken kept the upper hand or had some kind of agreement with those opponents, then I can imagine some coordinated effort to keep all that stuff underground, but most antagonists don't have any desire to do that. </div> </div> </div>They do. There's very little in the way of things in Werewolf that exist to tear down the masquerade except when it's convenient. A claimed may want to be worshiped but doing so outside of a small commune its already got a grasp on is likely to shorten its lifespan. Also the Forsaken effectively exist to destroy the rare thing that does try to 'break' the masquerade.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This doesn't even take into account how Forsaken themselves can screw things up. Like, imagine pack succumbing to Kuruth in the middle of the city - something which is likely to happen sooner or later in the history of setting. Even if thousands of people succumb to Lunacy, amount of material evidence left behind in slaughter would be too great to play the good old &quot;terrorist attack&quot; card. How do you treat this issue in your games? </div> </div> </div>Harmony is a lot easier to get a handle on in second edition, to the point that, while I won't say it's not a concern, the idea of someone, let alone an entire pack, spontaneously and unknowingly raging in the middle of a city is less likely to happen. Elodoth are capable of shutting down rages, there's the Chain Rage rite, Still Small Voice gift, just regular Resolve and Composure, a generalized awareness of your triggers and an encouragement to achieve a level of balance, as well as a lot of signs for when someone is out of balance.<br /> <br /> And even then, if there's a series of cascade failures and the most irresponsible and loose cannon pack goes loose cannon, there's a variety of ways to make any material evidence worthless. Using Black Earth, Red Hunger can eradicate any traces of bodies. The Veil rite can destroy any recorded evidence of the act. If someone wants they can use Command Artifice or Iron Slave and drop a plane to crash on the area, or use Tongue of Flame and Catastrophe and turn it into an inferno.<br /> <br /> This all just goes to say that it's not as bad as you are making out. There's more elements in place to maintain the masquerade than destroy it, killing isn't as commonplace, kuruth isn't as hair trigger, and evidence can be destroyed. But if someone wants a major masquerade breach in their area to have an impact, I'd suggest rather than 'People saw a video of werewolves and now everyone believes in werewolves and the spirit world' they instead focus on the werewolf-oriented ramifications. Namely, all the Lunacy is going to lead to a lot of people with serious problems, whatever pack caused it is likely to either take care of things themselves (like Die Gaedestrassebande in the Pack) or be taken care of by others (Blood Talons), which can lead to werewolves in the area trying to make extra sure that it doesn't happen again, or if it does it is contained. This can target 'loose cannon' werewolves, it can involve factions taking over local media (Iron Masters and Ivory Claws in particular are in prime position to become network or news owners). A piece of inspiration might be the fiction in the core of second edition, which involves a truly massive level of destruction that would likely never be tied to anything supernatural (probably going down in history as a crime spree followed by an epidemic) but the focus is what the characters are going to do about it.<br /> <br /> Added: For a smaller break, especially in the PCs territory or something that is their fault, a hunt to destroy evidence makes for a pretty spot on session or story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 05-15-2021, 05:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T13:55:51-07:00","id":1448619,"title":"","content":"The answer will vary by campaign to campaign and what sub-genres and tones you're going for, but there's three basic dials to play with: baseline supernatural population, drama frequency, and mortal response.<br /> <br /> The first dial, supernatural population, is based on how you scale the frequency of supernatural beings. Spirits are relatively numerous, but Spirits that can interact with the material world are rare. Hosts are rare. Werewolves are rare. What you define as &quot;rare&quot; will have a big impact on how this comes across in-game. My own personal baseline is much lower numbers overall than the books seem to assume, but then again, the books are written in a manner designed to provoke rather than prevent your ideas and inspiration. &quot;There aren't any vampires in Cleveland&quot; might be good for the logic of the world, but not good for people who want to set a game in Cleveland. If your home setting is well-populated, add a Hellmouth to justify it and establish that most of the world, except for other Hellmouth-zones, are relatively barren of supernatural beings. This is easily done in Werewolf by making key loci and verges rare, such that a place with enough to justify more than 0 werewolves are rare and places with enough spirit activity to support multiple packs of werewolves <i>extremely </i>rare.<br /> <br /> The second dial is how frequently supernatural beings end up in player-character style dramatic situations. Just like the real world, even people with &quot;interesting&quot; jobs spend most of their time mostly doing nothing particularly interesting. Vampires could go decades between weird factional strife that results in car bombings and Masquerade-stretching events. Player characters are expected to always be living interesting lives, but the vast majority of werewolves probably don't have that much to do most of the time that relates directly to, well, being a werewolf. Most Hunts are routine, just like most plane landings are routine. Again the books are written as if shit is always going down all the time everywhere, but that's the style. So yeah, the Pure <i>as a whole</i> probably kill a lot of people, but that doesn't mean every individual Pure is always killing a lot of people a lot of the time. A related part of the puzzle is how frequency the drama results in secondary evidence, witnesses, secret societies devoted to worshipping the fallen angel that landed in the community pool, etc. These events are even rarer; vampires keep their feuding confined to conveniently empty warehouse districts in the middle of the night. Werewolves fight their battles deep in the forest, well away from the hiking trails. That's before the various Men in Black and Conspiracies of Silence show up.<br /> <br /> The third dial is mortal response. You get a bit of a safety cushion with Lunacy and the Sleeping Curse, but at the end of the day, this is a genre conceit. Secret monsters remain secret because part of the fantasy of &quot;urban fantasy&quot; is you get to mix the familiar and mundane with the exotic and unnatural. So if you're wondering how they eat and breathe, and other science facts, remember it's just a show, you should really just relax. In addition to the safety cushion, you don't need to assume the masquerade or the veil are actually that rigid. People can <i>know</i>, they can <i>suspect</i>, as individuals, as organizations, as whole communities or institutions. Hunter's whole premise is, after all, that some people have some idea of what's going on, some of the time. Their reactions just need to funnel towards keeping the secret. So torch-bearing mobs burn the evidence and swear to never speak of what they saw the night the town attacked Old Woman Witchy's farmhouse after all those kids vanished. Most of them don't think about it, or try not to, but that doesn't stop you from revealing things in deathbed confessions, secret journals, coded notes, oddly fragmentary audio log scattered around the abandoned science laboratory, etc. Conspiracies of monster hunters keep the secret; they join the night's unspoken promise Not To Talk About it. But just like Fight Club, this rule is violated constantly; it just never percolates to the &quot;general public&quot; beyond the level of rumor, legend, shameful taboos, and whispers.<br /> <br /> Heck, people can believe (and probably do!) in Werewolves, just like people believe in UFOs, or Qanon, or chiropractors, or Delaware. But it's kind of like the Masquerade in Requiem; it doesn't really matter if some of the population believes vaguely there might be vampires out there, or another fraction of the population has the intuition that there are certain night clubs that are more dangerous than others, and so forth. It matters if your neighbors know <i>you </i>are a vampire. And once they <i>know</i>, whatever caused them to cross that threshold, suddenly they too keep the secret. Occultists, cultists, conspirators, hunters, mystery seekers, sorcerers, psychics, everyone who has a &quot;character in a horror novel&quot; premise has some handwavey genre-conceit roadblock stopping them from assembling into the critical mass of <i>knowing </i>necessary to change society in a visible way; the conceit of secret monsters in the modern world survives. Unless you want to do a masquerade breach storyline, at which point it shatters however you darn well please.<br /> <br /> For the TLDR:<br /> <br /> 1) supernatural beings are rare<br /> 2) supernatural drama that leaves behind evidence is uncommon<br /> 3) humans that find out inevitably join the cover-up for whatever reason<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 05-15-2021, 08:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T14:31:10-07:00","id":1448626,"title":"","content":"Oh, there's a fourth dial you can play with. I'll call it &quot;Inertia.&quot; A lot of breaches to the Masquerade wouldn't necessarily result in widespread social change; they wouldn't actually matter all that much.<br /> <br /> Imagine if tomorrow Bigfoot turned out to be real. Straight-up, full stop, a near extinct race of near-human hominids in the northwestern United States. Do you stop going to work the next day? Does the country invade Iraq? Are new federal agencies spun up to manage the Bigfoot? Does the economy crash? How much actual impact does this have on day-to-day life; are there new religions, do wars begin, are diseases cured? What happens to the price of Bitcoin?<br /> <br /> Probably not, right? Maybe Oregon's wildlife management department gets a bigfoot budget.<br /> <br /> So say we've got a full fledged Masquerade-breach event at a city. The Mayor, the Police Chief, the Medical Examiner, and an Intrepid Reporter are all reviewing the evidence and they come to the inescapable conclusion that it was a werewolf attack. The genre demands that they <i>keep it a secret</i>; maybe they have to blackmail Kolchak at the end to pull this off, but in general, they'll hush it up on their own. That's <i>before </i>Task Force Valkyrie shows up offering bribes and threats, that's before the Invictus shows up to tamper with memories, etc. Now maybe these guys form the kernel of a new Hunter cell - that's a fun premise! - or a secret society because they want to become werewolves or get revenge or whatever. Lets say they resist the instinct to cover it up and they're too well connected or protected or lucky or whatever to be Men in Black'd.<br /> <br /> So they present the evidence. It's pretty conclusive, though these days, anyone can disbelieve anything, or believe anything. What actually <i>changes</i>, in an immediate sense? Do people still talk about it three news cycles later? What's the actionable follow-up if there's no way to reliably detect werewolves and the werewolves themselves go &quot;oh shit&quot; and keep things on the down-low for a while? There's no active threat to human society, no profitable business opportunities in the exciting new field of werewolf management, no avenues for scientific glory.....if you set the &quot;Inertia&quot; dial to &quot;High&quot;, the general churn of human society overcomes transitory breaches in the Masquerade, as long as they do not last. Sure, if a werewolf army conquers Seattle, then yeah, it's over for the Masquerade as the US government commandeers silver and gets Lockheed-Martin to develop a lycanthrope-buster-bomb.<br /> <br /> If you set it to &quot;Low&quot; then, yeah, singular breach events can pop the whole thing, or lots and lots of smaller events accumulating. But there's nothing stopping you from saying that the big picture of a superficial resemblance to the real world can regenerate itself even when very public unnatural events sometimes occur. People are jaded, people want to go back to normal, they're afraid of the consequences of probing too deep, there's too much power invested in secrecy, magic disguises itself over time, the God-Machine intervenes, and at the end of the day, lots of people believe in ghosts. Very few people learn the Rite of Mot and travel town-to-town in a vintage car destroying ghosts at the secret behest of a real estate company.<br /> <br /> EDIT: All that being said, a short-lived moral panic over a supernatural event is a fun idea for a scenario. Local news gets obsessed over stuff that isn't actually happening all the time, it's not that much of a stretch to adapt something like the &quot;Knockout Game!&quot; moral panic and extend it to &quot;Are your teenagers biting each other to drink their blood? Stay tuned!&quot; stuff that derives from a real vampire fuckup but gets translated into a mundane, short-lived, media-driven sensation. The second-order misinformation gets debunked, the original (real) supernatural event gets buried deep into the part of the wikipedia article no one reads.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 05-16-2021, 04:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T14:18:30-07:00","id":1448717,"title":"","content":"When I hear the acronym for forsaken I think your saying WTF?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T15:41:27-07:00","id":1448720,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s important to remember in Chronicles of Darkness that the point of the Masquerade is not keep mortals from knowing the supernatural exists. They know. It\u2019s to prevent them from knowing that you specifically are a supernatural that Hunters might target.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T16:01:03-07:00","id":1448722,"title":"","content":"In modern society, I think the concept of a Masquerade, or the human race being ignorant of all brands of supernaturals is just a suspension of disbelief at this point. Even for a game set in in the 90s and early 00s (before the advent of smartphones in nearly every human's hands), the idea of human society not being aware of vampires or werewolves or mages etc sort of stretches credulity. Realistically, a place like the Chronicles of Darkness would have turned into something like True Blood a while ago.<br /> <br /> My point is basically, 'a superficial explanation of why humans don't know is enough to make these stories work, the setting already relies on a narrative fiat of Humans Don't Know.'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T16:24:32-07:00","id":1448724,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vintervalpen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1448603-how-masquerade-is-kept-in-wtf?p=1448722#post1448722\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In modern society, I think the concept of a Masquerade, or the human race being ignorant of all brands of supernaturals is just a suspension of disbelief at this point. Even for a game set in in the 90s and early 00s (before the advent of smartphones in nearly every human's hands), the idea of human society not being aware of vampires or werewolves or mages etc sort of stretches credulity. Realistically, a place like the Chronicles of Darkness would have turned into something like True Blood a while ago.<br /> <br /> My point is basically, 'a superficial explanation of why humans don't know is enough to make these stories work, the setting already relies on a narrative fiat of Humans Don't Know.'</div> </div> </div> </div>Mage accounts for people\u2019s unrealistic levels of denial via the Sleeper Curse. But the fact is that people know in broad strokes, supernaturals just end to avoid exposing themselves as individuals.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T16:43:10-07:00","id":1448725,"title":"","content":"To expand on what others are saying and pointing out that it's not just supernaturals that want the masquerade, the introduction from the first World of Darkness book gets into it.<br /> <br /> World of Darkness p15 &quot;Everyone has had the sense at least once in their lives that things are not right with the world, that not everything is at it seems. We sometimes feel that sinister truths hide behind a fa\u00e7ade of normality, veiled partially by the rational, orderly \u201cnatural laws\u201d taught to us by science. We\u2019re told that medieval beliefs in monsters and magic were merely primitive superstitions. We\u2019re too wise for that sort of foolishness these days. Or so we assure ourselves. But at night, when the shadows grow long and the wind whistles through the trees, we shudder and remember older truths, the truths of our ancestors, who were right to fear the dark.<br /> We know deep down that the world is a far more terrifying place than we allow our rational minds to acknowledge. To accept this subconscious truth is to invite madness, to succumb to the raw chaos that lurks at the edges of our perception. Best to shut our eyes, pretend it\u2019s not there. If we don\u2019t see it, it might not see us.<br /> Pretending something is not there, however, does not make it go away. It only helps it to hide better \u2014 and predators like to hide from their prey, lest it be scared away.<br /> A world where such predators truly existed is a conspiracy theorist\u2019s worst nightmare. In such a reality, unseen beings hatch incognito plots against us, pulling our strings like puppeteers looming above us, hidden in the darkness beyond the stage lights. Our only protection is our ignorance, the obliviousness that allows us to keep going day by day, building toward \u201csomething meaningful\u201d \u2014 a career, a home, a family. Allegations about secret masters or creatures lurking in the night simply lack evidence. If these things are real, why don\u2019t we see them on the evening news? Even Internet sites dedicated to exposing unknown forces in our lives can\u2019t produce a single, verifiable picture. It\u2019s hard to believe in something we can\u2019t see.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T17:01:16-07:00","id":1448726,"title":"","content":"Mage has two generally useful supernatural phenomena or concepts to apply, as well. The Sleeping Curse - the tendency to rationalize, forget, dismiss, ignore, and otherwise deny the supernatural. While I hate how it is mechanically implemented in Mage, as a background concept, it's basically load-bearing if you want to dress up the genre conceit of secret monsters with some plausible thaumobabble.<br /> <br /> There's also Pancryptia, the tendency of magic itself to conceal, to become occult. Assume it broadly applies to the supernatural rather than just supernal magic and it applies to the human response. All the stuff that gets past the Sleeping Curse (because there are plenty of ways around it) still runs into Pancryptia; people form secret societies and secret agencies. They encipher their grimoires and cover-up their activities. They form cults and cells and conspiracies, not Facebook groups, mass movements, or explanatory YouTube channels with a distinctive art style. There's a deep-seated taboo about talking about the &quot;Real Deal&quot; openly. Whispers, rumors, secret messages and encoded symbolism, sure, but it's too...embarrassing, too shameful, to talk about in public. Doing so is a quick career and reputation destroyer. Internet discussion of the supernatural is ubiquitous; there are forums full of people who &quot;believe in vampires.&quot; But the stuff where people whoactually have faced the real deal is deeply encrypted and anonymous.<br /> <br /> Anything people are comfortable talking about openly is Not the Real Deal. Talking about the Real Deal is done privately, in masked cult ceremonies, via number station broadcasts between shadow agencies, on burner phones and in safehouses, away from home and work. <br /> <br /> This also lets you preserve the general slurry of real-world occult nonsense as the outer layer of hoaxes and misinformation and such. Aleister Crowley, the historical figure, can be the same exact douchebag in the WoD as he was in real life. Your classic occult bookstore with real occult knowledge for sale, a good fun WoD trope, puts the fake stuff in the front. Public facing it's just a New Age bookstore for when you want to eat kale and wear crystals. If you know the right password, though, the owner might make a phone call, and a private dealer might meet you in the basement to haggle over the price of the <i>True Death of Samuel</i>, which has some real information on Canaanite Necromancy...<br /> <br /> EDIT: I should also say that the books aren't necessarily that good about having this discussion, since they are by different authors with different interpretations of how the setting works, ranging from satirical excess to extreme austerity, and the default impulse is to say &quot;yes!&quot; and fill them with Stuff Happening (TM). If every crazy murderous thing from all the books was happening at once, the entire CofD world would be an action sequence from the Underworld films, all the time. Be selective!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 05-16-2021, 08:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T17:29:26-07:00","id":1448731,"title":"","content":"If all else fails, this is literally an example in the book of how to get your next dot of Purity:<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.pinimg.com/originals/32/78/c4/3278c4e550b505c3c2368b4212f9775d.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-17T22:16:00-07:00","id":1448832,"title":"","content":"1. Humankind knows.<br /> <br /> 2. But, like, they don't <i>know</i> know.<br /> <br /> This is basically a dial that you can turn. At setting 0, absolutely no one has a hint of Uratha besides the &quot;prey psychology&quot; baked into every human. At setting 11, the government has public research labs backing dedicated kill squads that specialize in Uratha and politicians run for election with the campaign promise to stamp them out. You can absolutely do both: Forsaken is a game about being the underdog despite being an apex predator, and it works just as well if you lean into one as the other; sure, you can't do every story on any setting, but you can't do every story anyways.<br /> <br /> For figuring it out in your world, you need to get specific. <b>Who</b> knows? Is it the kid down the street who saw some shit? An ambitious reporter who's eager for a scoop? An occultist trying for immortality? A police commissioner who wants to get her crime stats down? The mayor, governor, or prime minister? <b>What</b> do they know? Do they think there's a vigilante running around who gets really gory? Are they aware of Uratha pack structure and how they organize into larger groups? Are they aware of Hosts? Do they know there's also a spirit world beyond the veil? Do they care because it's an avenue to power? Did they see a man-beast? An overly intelligent giant wolf? Did they witness shapeshifting?<br /> <br /> How do they feel about this information? What do they want to do with it? Specifically, what do they want, that this evidence helps them with?<br /> <br /> A lot of people will sweep the evidence under the rug because telling the truth probably means a target on their back. Differently, a lot of Uratha-human relations are probably cordial overall; because anti-human Anshega are probably going to <i>avoid</i> humans, rather than antagonize them; and because Urdaga are generally going to be trying to improve the territory, which, on the whole, is going to look a lot like improving things for the humans. If you saw the guy who just shut down the order to bulldoze your favorite park turn into a wolf, are you going to turn him in?<br /> <br /> Are the governments probably collecting a lot of subtle data points pointing to the clear existence of Uratha? Probably. But why tell anyone? I'm not saying they wouldn't: see setting 11 above: but really think: <i>why</i> are they telling people? It doesn't have to be a sinister reason. Public safety is a pretty obvious one, and justifying the militarization of the police is much more gray area one. Given this, how do they pursue their investigation? If it's about public health and safety, then they might focus on wolf bites and restricting the kinds of dogs people may own: much better than panicking people about monsters in their midst.<br /> <br /> tl;dr, there's a lot of steps between &quot;something goes bump in the night&quot; and &quot;welp, silver bomb the whole city&quot;. At the very least, you have street-level concerns, crazy occultist concerns, anyone compiling some kind of encyclopedia of Uratha, and the government knowing or having an opinion about the matter, and there's plenty of creative room at every level. Don't rely on, &quot;it must have happened at some point:&quot; get specific, explain <i>what</i> happened and <i>who</i> saw it and what conclusions they drew from that.<br /> <br /> And at the end of the day, it's enough if you and your players can suspend their disbelief. If you can't, then sure, figure this out. But if you can, and it's not what you want your story to be about, then does it really matter?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":2758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","user":"Errol216","date":". 05-18-2021, 01:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-20T07:06:59-07:00","id":1449112,"title":"","content":"The United States has somewhere between about six and seven thousand unsolved murders every year, and it can probably be assumed that a certain quantity of murders that are marked down as solved are associated with people that the justice system railroaded. Also several hundred thousand unresolved disappearances.<br /> <br /> I think the human or human adjacent targets of Uratha predation in that particular country can be slotted into those numbers a bit.<br /> <br /> Other countries might have less such death (although probably a bit higher in this setting than in reality), but that might be pegged to lower werewolf numbers and perhaps a somewhat less overbearing Shadow.<br /> <br /> Plus, it's my understanding that even when humans are designated as the targets of a Sacred Hunt, the resolution doesn't strictly need to involve killing them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}